  "assembly": "# Advent of Code 2025, day 5 part 1 (Cafeteria)\n#\n# THE PUZZLE\n# The input is a list of ranges of fresh ingredient IDs (3-5 means 3, 4 and 5; ranges may overlap) and\n# a list of available IDs. The answer is how many of the available IDs lie in at least one range.\n#\n# THE IDEA\n# The IDs have up to 15 decimal digits, more than the 32 bits of a register. So every number is kept as\n# four 16-bit words, and two numbers are compared like two words in a dictionary: look at the top words;\n# only when they are equal look at the next ones. The first pair that differs decides.\n#     is 17 in 16-20?     17 against 16: top words 0 = 0, 0 = 0, 0 = 0, then 17 > 16: not below the range\n#                         17 against 20: top words 0 = 0, 0 = 0, 0 = 0, then 20 > 17: not above it: fresh\n# With the real input the second pair of words nearly always decides, so a test of one ID against one\n# range is short. Each ID is tested against the ranges in the order they come and the search stops at\n# the first range that holds it, so an ID in two ranges is counted once.\n#\n# THE WHOLE PROGRAM IN PSEUDO CODE\n#     COUNT = 0\n#     for each ID X in the list:                # the list ends at a number whose top word is 0xFFFF\n#         for each range LOW-HIGH:\n#             if X < LOW: continue              # compared from the top word down\n#             if X > HIGH: continue\n#             if HIGH's top word is 0xFFFF: break       # the end mark of the ranges: X is spoiled\n#             COUNT = COUNT + 1; break                  # X is fresh\n#     write COUNT as five decimal digits\n#     stop\n#\n# THE END OF THE RANGES COSTS NOTHING\n# After the last range comes one more, from 0 to the largest number there is (every word 0xFFFF). Every\n# ID is \"in\" it, so the search always stops there at the latest, and no test for the end of the list is\n# needed while searching. Only when an ID is found in a range does the program look whether that range\n# is the end mark.\n#\n# THE RESULT, when the CPU stops on the Breakpoint at END\n#   0x0100         the count as one 16-bit word\n#   0x0110-0x0114  the count as five decimal digits, one per word, most significant first\n#\n# THE INPUT (rule 1 of RULES.md)\n# A number is four 16-bit words, lowest word first. 484155089502467 = 0x0001_B856_1F05_6503 is stored as\n# 0x6503; 0x1F05; 0xB856; 0x0001.\n#   0x2000-  the ranges, eight words each: LOW, then HIGH. Then the end mark: 0, 0, 0, 0 and four 0xFFFF.\n#   0x3000-  the IDs, four words each. Then the end mark: four words 0xFFFF.\n# Built-in limits: every number is below 0xFFFF_0000_0000_0000 (so its top word is never 0xFFFF); at\n# most 511 ranges (0x2000-0x2FFF with the end mark); the count stays below 65,536; in a range, LOW is\n# not above HIGH.\n#\n# ONE WORD AT A TIME\n# The registers only ever hold one 16-bit word here, so the upper halves stay 0 and A - B never goes\n# wrong. The idioms used everywhere (v is a variable's address, below 0x1000):\n#     getA v                   A = the word at v   (lia v; load rgA rgA: lia puts the address in A, load replaces it)\n#     getB v                   B = the word at v\n#     putA v                   the word at v = A   (this uses up B)\n#     getAoff p k              A = the word at the address (p + k); p is a variable holding an address\n#     if_eq L / if_neq L       compares A with B as they are now\n#     sub; if_carry L          after A - B: taken when A >= B\n# What each instruction does exactly is in findings/cpu-instructions.md.\n#\n# MEMORY MAP\n#   0x0100-0x012C  variables            0x1E00-  POWERS: 10000, 1000, 100, 10, 1, 0\n#   0x1000-        the program          0x2000-  the ranges (187 and the end mark end at 0x25DF)\n#   0xFF00         where the CPU starts 0x3000-  the IDs (1000 and the end mark end at 0x3FA3)\n\n# ---- The CPU's instructions as symbols (day 1's table without its _32 names, which the CPU ignores) ----\nsymbol nop 0b0000_0000_0000_0000            # the word 0: stops the CPU (the same word as non)\nsymbol ld  0b0010_0000_0000_0000            # load a register from memory (used through the load macro)\nsymbol lia 0b0011_0000_0000_0000            # lia n: A = n (0 to 4095), upper half 0\nsymbol lib 0b0100_0000_0000_0000            # lib n: B = n (0 to 4095), upper half 0\nsymbol sto 0b0101_0000_0000_0000            # store a register to memory (used through the store macro)\n\nmacro  load  { ld  | a? | (b? >> 6) }       # load X Y: X = the word at the address in Y\nmacro  store { sto | a? | (b? >> 6) }       # store X Y: the word at the address in Y = X\n\nsymbol sum    0b0110_0100_0000_0000         # A = A + B (32 bits), sets zero, carry, nega\nsymbol sub    0b0110_0010_0000_0000         # A = A - B (32 bits), sets zero, carry, nega\nsymbol shf    0b0110_0001_0000_0000         # shf | k: A = A shifted left by k (0 to 15)\nsymbol and    0b0110_0000_1000_0000         # A = A and B\nsymbol or     0b0110_0000_0100_0000         # A = A or B\nsymbol xor    0b0110_0000_0010_0000         # A = A xor B\nsymbol neg    0b0110_0000_0001_0000         # A = every bit of A flipped\n\nsymbol psh 0b1010_0000_0000_0000            # psh X: push the 16 bits of X\nsymbol pop 0b1011_0000_0000_0000            # pop X: pop 16 bits into X\n\nsymbol jmp  0b1100_0000_0000_0000           # jmp; L: go to L.  jmp c; L: go to L when c holds\nsymbol call 0b1110_0000_0000_0000           # call; L: run the subroutine at L\nsymbol ret  0b1111_0000_0000_0000           # return from a subroutine\n\n# The registers, as load, store, psh and pop name them\nsymbol rgAH 0b1000_0000_0000                # the upper 16 bits of A\nsymbol rgA  0b0100_0000_0000                # the lower 16 bits of A\nsymbol rgBH 0b0010_0000_0000                # the upper 16 bits of B\nsymbol rgB  0b0001_0000_0000                # the lower 16 bits of B\nsymbol rgSP 0b0000_1000_0000                # the program counter (leave alone)\nsymbol rgSB 0b0000_0100_0000                # the stack pointer (leave alone)\nsymbol non  0                               # stops the CPU (Breakpoint): the end of the program\n\n# The conditions of jmp\nsymbol zero  0b1000_0000_0000               # the last sum or sub gave 0\nsymbol carry 0b0100_0000_0000               # the last sum carried, or the last sub did not borrow (A >= B)\nsymbol nega  0b0010_0000_0000               # the last sub went below 0 (A < B)\nsymbol eq    0b0001_0000_0000               # A == B right now\nsymbol neq   0b0000_1000_0000               # A != B right now\n\n# ---- Macros for the common operations (explained in the README). A macro cannot use another macro, so\n#      load and store are spelled out here. v: a variable, p: a variable holding an address, L: a label ----\nmacro getA     { lia a?; ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6) }                                              # A = v\nmacro getB     { lib a?; ld | rgB | (rgB >> 6) }                                              # B = v\nmacro putA     { lib a?; sto | rgA | (rgB >> 6) }                                             # v = A (uses B)\nmacro getAat   { lia a?; ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6); ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6) }                       # A = the word p points at\nmacro putAat   { lib a?; ld | rgB | (rgB >> 6); sto | rgA | (rgB >> 6) }                      # the word p points at = A (uses B)\nmacro inc      { lia a?; ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6); lib 1; sum; lib a?; sto | rgA | (rgB >> 6) }  # v = v + 1 (uses A and B)\nmacro goto     { jmp; a? }                                                                    # go to L\nmacro if_eq    { jmp eq; a? }                                                                 # go to L when A == B\nmacro do       { call; a? }                                                                   # call the subroutine L\nmacro if_carry { jmp carry; a? }                                                              # go to L after a sub when A >= B\nmacro getAoff  { lia a?; ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6); lib b?; sum; ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6) }                # A = the word at (p + k) (uses B)\nmacro if_neq   { jmp neq; a? }                                                                # go to L when A != B\n\n# ---- Variables (all below 0x1000, so lia/lib can address them) ----\n# The result\npointer COUNT 0x0100 0\n# ID: the address of the ID being tested. X0-X3: a copy of its four words, X3 the top one.\npointer ID 0x0120 0x3000\npointer X0 0x0121 0\npointer X1 0x0122 0\npointer X2 0x0123 0\npointer X3 0x0124 0\n# R: the address of the range being tested (LOW is at R to R + 3, HIGH at R + 4 to R + 7)\npointer R 0x0125 0\n# For the decimal digits: what is left of the count, the current power of ten, how often it fitted\npointer REST 0x0126 0\npointer POWER 0x0127 0\npointer DIGIT_VALUE 0x0128 0\n# Pointers: DIGIT the next digit to write, POW the next power of ten\npointer DIGIT 0x0129 0x0110\npointer POW 0x012A 0x1E00\n# Constants that do not fit an instruction's place for a number\npointer RANGES 0x012B 0x2000\npointer END_MARK 0x012C 0xFFFF\n\n# ---- The CPU starts at 0xFF00 ----\norigin 0xFF00\ngoto MAIN\n\norigin 0x1000\n@ MAIN\n# ==== take the next ID ====\n#     X3, X2, X1, X0 = the four words at ID\n#     if X3 == 0xFFFF: goto ALL_IDS_DONE        # the end mark of the IDs\n#     R = RANGES\n@ NEXT_ID\n\tgetAoff ID 3; putA X3                                        # X3 = the ID's top word\n\tgetB END_MARK                                                # B = 0xFFFF\n\tif_eq ALL_IDS_DONE                                           # the end mark: no more IDs\n\tgetAoff ID 2; putA X2                                        # X2 = the ID's third word\n\tgetAoff ID 1; putA X1                                        # X1 = the ID's second word\n\tgetAat ID; putA X0                                           # X0 = the ID's lowest word\n\tgetA RANGES; putA R                                          # R = the first range\n\n# ==== is X below this range? ====\n#     for k in 3, 2, 1, 0:                      # from the top word down\n#         if memory[R + k] != Xk: goto LOW_DIFFERS\n#     goto TEST_HIGH                            # X == LOW\n#   LOW_DIFFERS:\n#     if memory[R + k] > Xk: goto NEXT_RANGE    # X < LOW\n@ TEST_LOW\n\tgetAoff R 3; getB X3                                         # A = LOW's top word, B = X's\n\tif_neq LOW_DIFFERS\n\tgetAoff R 2; getB X2                                         # the third words\n\tif_neq LOW_DIFFERS\n\tgetAoff R 1; getB X1                                         # the second words\n\tif_neq LOW_DIFFERS\n\tgetAat R; getB X0                                            # the lowest words\n\tif_neq LOW_DIFFERS\n\tgoto TEST_HIGH                                               # all four equal: X == LOW\n@ LOW_DIFFERS\n\tsub                                                          # A = LOW's word - X's word (they differ)\n\tif_carry NEXT_RANGE                                          # LOW's word is larger: X < LOW\n\n# ==== is X above this range? ====\n#     for k in 3, 2, 1, 0:\n#         if memory[R + 4 + k] != Xk: goto HIGH_DIFFERS\n#     goto IN_RANGE                             # X == HIGH\n#   HIGH_DIFFERS:\n#     if memory[R + 4 + k] > Xk: goto IN_RANGE  # X < HIGH\n#     goto NEXT_RANGE                           # X > HIGH\n@ TEST_HIGH\n\tgetAoff R 7; getB X3                                         # A = HIGH's top word, B = X's\n\tif_neq HIGH_DIFFERS\n\tgetAoff R 6; getB X2                                         # the third words\n\tif_neq HIGH_DIFFERS\n\tgetAoff R 5; getB X1                                         # the second words\n\tif_neq HIGH_DIFFERS\n\tgetAoff R 4; getB X0                                         # the lowest words\n\tif_neq HIGH_DIFFERS\n\tgoto IN_RANGE                                                # all four equal: X == HIGH\n@ HIGH_DIFFERS\n\tsub                                                          # A = HIGH's word - X's word (they differ)\n\tif_carry IN_RANGE                                            # HIGH's word is larger: X < HIGH\n\n# ==== the next range ====\n#     R = R + 8\n@ NEXT_RANGE\n\tgetA R; lib 8; sum; putA R                                   # R = R + 8\n\tgoto TEST_LOW\n\n# ==== X is in the range at R ====\n#     if memory[R + 7] != 0xFFFF: COUNT = COUNT + 1     # a real range: X is fresh\n#     ID = ID + 4\n@ IN_RANGE\n\tgetAoff R 7; getB END_MARK                                   # A = HIGH's top word, B = 0xFFFF\n\tif_eq STEP_ID                                                # the end mark of the ranges: X is in no range\n\tinc COUNT                                                    # COUNT = COUNT + 1\n@ STEP_ID\n\tgetA ID; lib 4; sum; putA ID                                 # ID = ID + 4\n\tgoto NEXT_ID\n\n# ==== write COUNT as five decimal digits ====\n# A digit is how often a power of ten fits into what is left. Without division that is counting:\n#     REST = COUNT\n#     for POWER in 10000, 1000, 100, 10, 1:\n#         DIGIT_VALUE = 0\n#         while REST >= POWER: REST = REST - POWER; DIGIT_VALUE = DIGIT_VALUE + 1\n#         memory[DIGIT] = DIGIT_VALUE; DIGIT = DIGIT + 1\n# After sub, the carry flag is set when A >= B (no borrow).\n@ ALL_IDS_DONE\n\tgetA COUNT; putA REST                                        # REST = COUNT\n@ NEXT_POWER\n\tgetAat POW                                                   # A = memory[POW], the next power of ten\n\tputA POWER                                                   # POWER = A\n\tlib 0                                                        # B = 0\n\tif_eq END                                                    # a power of 0 ends the table\n\tinc POW                                                      # POW = POW + 1\n\tlia 0; putA DIGIT_VALUE                                      # DIGIT_VALUE = 0\n@ FITS_AGAIN\n\tgetA REST; getB POWER                                        # A = REST, B = POWER\n\tsub                                                          # A = REST - POWER\n\tif_carry FITS                                                # REST >= POWER: it fits once more\n\tgetA DIGIT_VALUE; putAat DIGIT                               # memory[DIGIT] = DIGIT_VALUE   (B holds the address in DIGIT)\n\tinc DIGIT                                                    # DIGIT = DIGIT + 1\n\tgoto NEXT_POWER\n@ FITS\n\tputA REST                                                    # REST = REST - POWER   (A still holds the difference)\n\tinc DIGIT_VALUE                                              # DIGIT_VALUE = DIGIT_VALUE + 1\n\tgoto FITS_AGAIN\n\n# The word 0 stops the CPU on its Breakpoint.\n@ END\n\tnon\n\n# ---- Powers of ten for the decimal digits, then 0 ----\norigin 0x1E00\n10000; 1000; 100; 10; 1; 0\n\n# ---- The ranges: 187, then the end mark ----\n# Eight words per range: LOW lowest word first, then HIGH lowest word first.\norigin 0x2000\n0x4103; 0x2420; 0xB856; 0x0001; 0x3080; 0x4213; 0xB8A9; 0x0001    # 484155089502467-484512074248320\n0xBECF; 0x925C; 0x29F9; 0x0001; 0x5196; 0xF85A; 0x2A2A; 0x0001    # 327626855857871-327839020372374\n0xD3D8; 0x6426; 0xE894; 0x0001; 0xC08B; 0x3894; 0xEC10; 0x0001    # 537199009780696-541029389615243\n0x2E6C; 0xDEA8; 0x26D0; 0x0001; 0x1282; 0x0212; 0x2700; 0x0001    # 324153507327596-324355964932738\n0x042F; 0x32AD; 0x9DC4; 0x0000; 0x8DBF; 0x42F6; 0x9E4A; 0x0000    # 173465989350447-174041788222911\n0x1B1A; 0x6DB9; 0xF7FC; 0x0000; 0x1B1A; 0x6DB9; 0xF7FC; 0x0000    # 272663544666906-272663544666906\n0x753B; 0x7815; 0x8B08; 0x0000; 0x47BE; 0xCCD0; 0x8C63; 0x0000    # 152868490671419-154360265852862\n0xAAB2; 0x0EBA; 0x3049; 0x0001; 0x18F0; 0x661B; 0x31A2; 0x0001    # 334565314570930-336048544225520\n0x5376; 0xFE71; 0x6104; 0x0000; 0xBD01; 0xC99B; 0x61CD; 0x0000    # 106674076603254-107536478616833\n0x5A11; 0x5BC4; 0x4A30; 0x0001; 0xD804; 0xF2DE; 0x4CA1; 0x0001    # 363046535191057-365733424846852\n0x6948; 0x508C; 0x82D5; 0x0000; 0x0BFE; 0x8CAB; 0x884A; 0x0000    # 143852691024200-14985376897